# Manage a JRebirth JPMS module-info

Every JRebirth client module ships a `module-info.java`. Get four things right: requires, exports, opens, and the ModuleStarter provider.

## Decide: open module or explicit opens

JRebirth core instantiates Model, View, and Controller by reflection, so their packages must be open to `org.jrebirth.af.core`.

- simplest: declare `open module name { ... }` so every package is open (showcases and the app shell do this).
- stricter: declare a plain `module` and add one `opens pkg to org.jrebirth.af.core;` per package that holds JRebirth components or FXML controllers.

## Required reads

- `requires org.jrebirth.af.api;` and `requires org.jrebirth.af.core;` at minimum.
- add `org.jrebirth.af.component`, `org.jrebirth.af.preloader`, and `org.jrebirth.af.iconfontbridge.*` only when used.
- JavaFX, JDK (`java.net.http`, `java.desktop`...), and third-party modules as the code needs; use `requires transitive` when an exported type leaks them.

## ModuleStarter provider (the one JRebirth provides)

```java
// XModuleStarter is generated by the JRebirth annotation processor at compile time.
provides org.jrebirth.af.api.module.ModuleStarter with your.pkg.XModuleStarter;
```

If the class is unresolved, the processor did not run: confirm the JRebirth processor is on the annotation-processor path, then rebuild. Do not hand-write the starter.

## ServiceLoader (uses / provides)

This is plain JPMS service injection, separate from JRebirth `@Register`/`@RegistrationPoint` (which is internal to JRebirth and needs no `provides`). Use `uses`/`provides` only for real `ServiceLoader` SPIs, e.g. a launcher `UIProvider`. SLF4J/Logback wire themselves at runtime; no `requires` is needed for the logback binding.

For step-by-step examples, the JavaFX requires matrix, resource opens, and troubleshooting, see `reference.md`.
Template: `templates/module-info.java.txt`.
